Q:- What is git.
""""""""""""""""
"Git is a version control system that tracks code changes and helps teams collaborate by managing different versions of a project.”

“Git is a distributed version control system that tracks changes in source code over time. It lets multiple developers work on the same project simultaneously, maintain a complete history of every change, create branches to develop features independently, and merge them back safely. Because it’s distributed, every developer has a full copy of the repository on their own machine, so they can work offline and the project isn’t dependent on a single central server. Platforms like GitHub, GitLab, and Bitbucket are used to host Git repositories and collaborate with teams.”

If they ask a follow-up, you can add:

Why use it? It helps you track history, roll back to an earlier version if something breaks, and collaborate without overwriting each other’s work.
Key commands: git init, git clone, git add, git commit, git push, git pull, git branch, git merge.
Git vs GitHub: Git is the tool that manages versions on your computer. GitHub is an online service that hosts Git repositories


Q:-Version Control
""""""""""""""""""
Version control maintains different snapshots of files/code over time, enabling tracking, rollback, and collaboration.
Demonstrated with a Google Sheets example: each edit is timestamped, and any prior version can be restored with one click.
Key benefits explained:
Tracking: Know who changed what and when.
Collaboration: Multiple developers can work in parallel from different locations.
Centralized management: Code pushed to a central repo is safe even if a developer's laptop fails or they go on leave.
Comparison: Diff between versions (who added/deleted what) is visible per commit.
Revert: A single git revert command restores a previous version (e.g., client rejects v2 and wants v1 back).
